From 3031b533bfd6bb87b0da7f574bd2920659630fba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 15:32:38 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 43/44] fix(chexc): normalization must not ask about a type
 parameter before its bounds exist

Coverage is "subtyping provable in the declaration's context, where-bounds included", so a
bounded parameter is covered by its bound in the same list. Normalization runs at producer
boundaries -- clause elaboration among them, which is before the bounds are attached to the
'GenericsTy' -- and asking there did more than answer wrongly: 'IsSubtype' keeps a cache that
outlives the phase, so one early 'false' made 'E <: IOException' invisible for the rest of the
compilation. That is a regression R3 introduced, and it reached beyond this rule: any capability
list mentioning a type parameter could poison the pair it asked about.

An entry whose parameter has no bounds yet is now kept without being compared, and is not offered
as a cover either. Subtyping through the bound works again -- an annotated binding and an
argument position both accept 'throws (E, IOE)' where 'throws IOE' is expected. The identity half
of the rule (the two spellings interning as one type) needs a normalization pass placed after the
bounds are attached, and stays open as I36.

Gate: compiler/Sema 3,514/3,514.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/Sema/TypeManager.cpp | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/Sema/TypeManager.cpp b/src/Sema/TypeManager.cpp
index ce6386d9..96c551d2 100644
--- a/src/Sema/TypeManager.cpp
+++ b/src/Sema/TypeManager.cpp
@@ -956,10 +956,29 @@ std::vector<Ptr<Ty>> TypeManager::NormalizeCapTys(const std::vector<Ptr<Ty>>& ca
         if (uncheckedExceptionTy && !cap->IsGeneric() && IsSubtype(cap, uncheckedExceptionTy)) {
             continue;
         }
+        // A type parameter whose bounds are not attached yet must not be COMPARED here. Coverage
+        // for such an entry is decided by its bounds ("coverage is subtyping provable in the
+        // declaration's context, 'where'-bounds included"), and normalization runs at producer
+        // boundaries -- clause elaboration among them, which happens before the bounds are on the
+        // 'GenericsTy'. Asking anyway answers 'false' AND caches it: 'IsSubtype' keeps a cache
+        // that outlives the phase, so one early question would make 'E <: IOException' false for
+        // the rest of the compilation. Such an entry is simply kept; a later normalization, when
+        // the bounds are known, folds it.
+        auto genericWithoutBounds = [](Ptr<Ty> ty) {
+            auto gTy = DynamicCast<GenericsTy*>(ty);
+            return gTy && gTy->upperBounds.empty();
+        };
+        if (genericWithoutBounds(cap)) {
+            if (!Utils::In(cap, kept)) {
+                kept.emplace_back(cap);
+            }
+            continue;
+        }
         // Keep the maximal entries. An entry equal to one already kept -- mutual subtypes -- is
         // dropped by the same test, which is what deduplication needs.
-        bool covered = std::any_of(capTys.begin(), capTys.end(),
-            [this, cap](Ptr<Ty> other) { return Ty::IsTyCorrect(other) && other != cap && IsSubtype(cap, other); });
+        bool covered = std::any_of(capTys.begin(), capTys.end(), [this, cap, &genericWithoutBounds](Ptr<Ty> other) {
+            return Ty::IsTyCorrect(other) && other != cap && !genericWithoutBounds(other) && IsSubtype(cap, other);
+        });
         if (!covered && !Utils::In(cap, kept)) {
             kept.emplace_back(cap);
         }
